 "WRSM_HESM_note": {
  "status": "no rerun (leader). WRSM/PMSM rows inside torque_speed_envelope_300kW_R01.json still use the T03 per-unit inputs and are NOT R01-valid.",
  "reactances": "R01 WRSM (5 mm gap): 2p8 L_d/L_q = 1.50/1.05 pu, 2p4 0.84/2.62 pu (inverse saliency), psi_f 48.3/47.6 mWb, field 2.3 kW, tau_f 0.29/0.26 s.",
  "T03_WRSM_verdict": "Weakened, not reversed. Characteristic current psi_f/L_d falls from about 240 A to about 173 A at 2p8 (field-weakening power limit about -30 %), while 2p4 rises from about 91 A to about 303 A (> I_max, i.e. unlimited field-weakening range in 2p4). The simulator gets T(2p4)/T(2p8) = 0.77 at 4500 rpm (analyst 0.42): no crossover inside the vehicle speed range, but one is plausible above it, and a 2p4 mode with inverse saliency uses positive-d reluctance torque, which T03 did not model. 'WRSM gains nothing from pole changing' holds for 4500 rpm max motor speed; it must be re-checked if the gear ratio is raised (cf. T03b R01: higher G favours machines with a strong high-speed mode).",
  "H09": "Turn halving in 2p4 (H09) acts on an already low L_d in 2p4; the benefit shrinks because 2p4 is no longer reactance-limited.",
  "H03_harmonic_excitation": "At the R01 5 mm gap the order-8 magnetising inductance falls by about g_eff 1.3 -> 5.3 mm (x0.25) and the field needs 2990 A-turns per pole (2.3 kW) instead of 737; the other analyst's capability (6.6 kW at the 1.3 mm gap) should drop roughly 4x to about 1.6 kW, below the 2.3 kW field need -> H03s likely infeasible at 5 mm unless the injection current budget rises. Needs their rerun (their gap sweep stops at 3 mm).",
  "T03c_HESM": "If T03c scaled the 8-pole inductances by k_w^2/p^2 for 2p4 (as T03 did), its 2p4 results are wrong in both axes (R01: inverse saliency); it should take L_d, L_q, field copper and tau_f from derived.wrsm, with the PM magnetic gap added. Not checked here.",
  "H10_H11": "Pulsation magnitudes for the WRSM k-sweep scale with the stator current; tau_f 0.29 s (not 45 ms) makes a k-sweep about 6x slower, i.e. the pulsation lasts longer but its amplitude is unchanged. The IM-based H11s result holds."
 }
